===Flooding===
{{Main|Aigües altes}}

[[File:Aigües altes.jpg|thumb|250px|Residents of Navassa during the [[w:2020 Brazilian floods and mudslides|floods]] of January 2020 in the Plaça Empurdá]]
The city is periodically threatened by high tides and heavy rainfall, which significantly raise the level of the [[Navassan Lagoon]]. These occurrences are referred to as ''Aigües altes'' ("High water"). The [[Confederal government of Balisca|confederal government]] in conjunction with the Navassan Cabildo has moved to head off this issue with the help water control officials who were behind the successful [[Mar Menor Works]] project on the [[Garrigàs Lake]], and the [[Augusta Storm Surge Barrier]], which protects [[Augustinian Coast]] from storm surge and sea-level rise.

Navassa was built with technologies borrowed by the Mesogeans colonists from other [[w:Thalassocracy|thalassocracies]], namely the [[Hesperian League]], and the [[Tartessia|Tartessian Republic]]. Volisanian settlers heavily utilized canals to drain and pump water out of the marshland, allowing them to extend development into lower elevation areas. Additionally, they used [[w:Levee|dikes]] ({{lang-ca|terraplè}}), to create a series of [[w:Polder|polders]], gradually extending the city into the Lagoon.

Today, around 1/3 of the city is at or below local mean sea level, while the other 2/3 is slightly above sea level. Evidence suggests that portions of the city may be dropping in elevation due to [[w:Subsidence|subsidence]]. The issue was brought into the national spotlight again in 2020, during the [[w:2020 Brazilian floods and mudslides|2020 Baliscan floods and landslides]] triggered by unprecedented concurrence of high tides, rain-swollen rivers and severe winds brought by [[w:South Atlantic tropical cyclone#Subtropical Storm Kurumí|Subtropical Storm Kurumí]]. In a 24-hour period, Kurumí dumped over 176 cm (around 7 inches) onto the city and simultaneously inundated the lower districts of the city. [[Pont de Sant Miquel]], the bridge linking [[L'Alcàsser]] (the historic old town) with [[Eixample, Navassa|Eixample]] was submerged, leaving the district isolated for 24 hours.

==Demographics==
===Ethnography===
{{See also|Ethnography of Balisca}}
In the [[2025 Baliscan census|2025 census]], nearly 30% of Navassa's population was foreign born, and around 50% of all children in the commune born to mothers who are immigrants. Similar to much of Balisca, immigrants to Navassa tend to originate from a single region in the world. The five largest sources of foreign-born individuals in the city as of 2019 were [[W:Spain|Spain]] (specifically {{W|Valencia}}, [[Catalonia]] and {{W|Andalusia}}), [[Marrascania]], [[France]], {{W|Haiti}}, and {{W|Senegal}}. Since 2016, the fastest growing foreign population have been of Nigerian (predominately [[W:Yoruba|Yoruba]] and [[W:Igbo|Igbo]]) origin. 

A 2015 study by researchers at [[University of Navassa]] and the [[University of Mesopotàmia]] determined that as many as 10,000 to 14,000 undocumented immigrants, many from {{W|Haiti}} and {{W|Venezuela}}, resided in Navassa. In 2020, the Pew Research Center estimated at least 35,000 undocumented immigrants lived in Navassa and its metropolitan area. The University of Navassa had been a {{W|sanctuary campus}} since 2009, and the the [[Assembly of Catharist Churches of Navassa]] (representing 27 {{W|Catharism|Catharist}} churches in the city) had declared themselves sanctuaries for {{W|undocumented immigrant}}s since 2012.
===Religion===
{{Further|Religion in Balisca}}
[[File:Basílica de Raymond Ajudant dels Càtars interior.png|alt=|left|thumb|300x300px|The [[Basílica de Raymond Ajudant dels Càtars]] in [[Empuria]]]]{{Pie chart|thumb=right|caption=Religion in Navassa (2025)|label3=[[Islam in Balisca|Islam]] (incl. {{W|Sunni}}, {{W|Shia}} and {{W|Liberalism and progressivism within Islam|Progressive}})|value3=18.4|color3=DarkGReen|label4={{W|African diaspora religions|African Diasporic}}|value4=5.7|color4=DarkOrchid|label1={{W|Irreligious}}|value1=42.4|color1=DodgerBlue|label2=[[Christianity in Balisca|Christianity]] (incl. [[Catharism]], {{W|Catholicism}}, [[w:Eastern Orthodox Church|Eastern]] and [[w:Oriental Orthodox Churches|Oriental Orthodox]])|value2=23.8|color2=Turquoise|label5={{W|Mythologies of the Indigenous peoples of the Americas|Indigenous}} beliefs|value5=2.5|color5=Chartreuse|label7=[[w:Hinduism|Hinduism]]|value7=1.5|color7=Orange|label8=[[w:Buddhism|Buddhism]]|value8=1.3|color8=Yellow|label6=[[w:Jewish people|Jewish]]|value6=2.2|color6=Hotpink|label9=Others/undeclared|value9=2.2|color9=Black}}Historically, Navassa has been predominantly [[Catharism|Catharist]], especially following the expulsion of [[w:Catholicism|Spanish and Aragonese Catholics]] in the early 17th century. This is clear from the large number of historical churches in the region, particularly in the district of [[Eixample, Navassa|Eixample]]. The pre-eminent Cathar [[w:Basilica|cathedral]] in Navassa is the [[Basílica de Raymond Ajudant dels Càtars]], serving as the [[w:Co-cathedral|co-cathedral]] of the [[Cathar Archdiocese of Saragossa-Navassa|Archdiocese of Saragossa–Navassa]]. In the northeast of the city, the [[Basilica of the Sacred Heart, Navassa|Basilica of the Sacred Heart]] is a Minor Basilica and parish church and the [[w:List of largest church buildings|28th largest church building]] in the world. The [[Church of Our Lady of Merixtell]] holds the tombs of many of the [[Viceroyalty of New Aragon|Viceroys of New Aragon]].

In reflection of its multicultural makeup, Navassa hosts a variety of religious communities, as well as large numbers of [[w:atheism|atheists]] and [[w:agnosticism|agnostics]]. Minority faiths include [[w:Islam|Islam]], [[W:Anglicanism|Anglicanism]], [[w:Eastern Orthodox Church|Eastern Orthodoxy]], [[w:Judaism|Judaism]], and [[w:Candomblé|Candomblé]]. According to a 2025 survey, approximately 42.4% of residents of Navassa declared themselves to be nonreligious, 12.4% [[Catharism|Cathar]] (9.3% were practising Cathars and 3.1% were non-practising Cathars), 11.4% were [[w:Roman Catholicism|Catholic]] (6.1% practising and 5.3% non-practising), 18.4% were [[Islam in Balisca|Muslim]] (11.8% practising, 6.6% non-practising), 5.7% were followers of an [[W:African diasporic religion|African diasporic religion]] (including [[Lucumí Tarica]] and [[W:Santería|Santería]]) and 9.7% were of another religion.
[[File:Cordoba House, Navassa.jpg|thumb|left|240px|The [[Cordoba House]] is a mosque and the seat of the Islamic and Cultural Centre of [[Mesopotàmia]].]]
Recognised religions and [[W:Secularism in Balisca|laicism]] enjoy public funding and school courses. It was once the case that every pupil in an official school from 6 years old to 18 had to choose 2 hours per week of compulsory religion—or laicist—inspired morals. However, this law was abolished in 1923, after the [[Confederal Council of Arbitration]] determined that religious studies should no longer be required in the primary and secondary education system.

Navassa has a sizable concentration of [[Islam in Balisca|Muslims]], mostly of [[Marrascania|Marrascesco]], [[W:Turks|Turkish]], [[W:Syrians|Syrian]] and [[W:Nigerians|Nigerian]] ancestry. The [[Grand Mosque of Navassa]], located in [[Sant Bernat Sesrovires]], is the oldest [[w:Mosque|mosque]] in Estuaria. Since Estuaria did not collect statistics by ethnic background prior to 1950, exact figures before then are unknown. It was determined that, in 2025, people of Muslim background living in the commune numbered 95,311 and accounted for 18.4% of the city's population, a figure that is relatively close to other cities in Eivissa.
